\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Task: Evidence-Grounded Document-Level Relation Extraction}
\label{sec:task}

Given a document $D = \{s_1, \ldots, s_n\}$ consisting of $n$ sentences and a set of entities $\mathcal{E}$ identified within $D$, the task of evidence-grounded document-level relation extraction (DocRE) requires predicting, for each entity pair $(e_h, e_t) \in \mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E}$, a relation $r \in \mathcal{R} \cup \{\texttt{NA}\}$ together with a set of supporting evidence sentences $E \subseteq D$. Standard DocRE predicts only the triple $(e_h, r, e_t)$; the evidence-grounded formulation also requires $E$, which grounds each prediction in specific textual support.

In our LLM-based approach, the model generates structured outputs that jointly encode both components. For each predicted relation, the output follows the format \texttt{(head, relation, tail) | Evidence: [S$i$][S$j$]}, requiring the model to produce complete evidence references inline with each triple rather than as a separate retrieval step. This design contrasts with encoder-based evidence methods such as SAIS~\citep{xiao2022sais} and DREEAM~\citep{ma2023dreeam}, which frame evidence identification as sentence-level binary classification. Because classification over a fixed sentence set does not increase output length, these methods incur no generation overhead. Our LLM formulation requires additional tokens for each evidence reference, creating a generation burden that scales with the number of predicted triples and their evidence sentences. This scale-dependent cost is the central phenomenon we investigate.


\subsection{Supervised Fine-Tuning Baseline}
\label{sec:sft}

We fine-tune all models with LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} at rank $r{=}64$ and scaling factor $\alpha{=}128$, applied to all linear projections (attention and feed-forward). The training data consists of DocRED~\citep{yao2019docred} training documents converted to the structured output format described above, where each example maps a document and its entity set to the complete set of relation triples with evidence annotations. We also train a \emph{no-evidence} variant under identical configuration but with evidence references removed from the output format, producing only \texttt{(head, relation, tail)}. This variant serves as an upper bound on extraction performance by eliminating the generation cost of evidence, and allows direct measurement of the evidence tax at each model scale. Detailed hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, number of epochs) are reported in the Appendix.


\subsection{Optimization Paradigms}
\label{sec:paradigms}

We evaluate three post-SFT optimization paradigms that differ in how they incorporate quality signals from the CED scoring function (\S\ref{sec:ced}).

\paragraph{GRPO.}
Group Relative Policy Optimization~\citep{shao2024deepseekmath} is an online RL method that eliminates the need for a separate value network by computing advantages within groups of sampled outputs. For each training document, the policy generates $G$ candidate responses, which are scored by CED. The advantage of each response is computed relative to the group mean, and the policy is updated to increase the probability of above-average outputs. We test two configurations: QLoRA with $G{=}4$ groups under 4-bit quantization, and bf16 full-precision LoRA with $G{=}8$. Because GRPO applies gradient updates that directly optimize the reward signal, evidence quality and extraction accuracy compete for gradient capacity. We analyze this multi-objective tension in Section~\ref{sec:spectrum}.

\paragraph{DPO.}
Direct Preference Optimization~\citep{rafailov2023direct} reformulates RLHF as a classification problem over preference pairs, without explicit reward models. We construct preference data from the SFT model's sampled outputs: for each document, the response with the highest CED score is labeled as preferred and the lowest-scoring response as rejected. DPO then adjusts the policy to increase the log-probability ratio between preferred and rejected completions. We train for 1 and 3 epochs to observe the temporal dynamics of evidence-extraction trade-offs under implicit optimization.

\paragraph{RSFT.}
Rejection Sampling Fine-Tuning is a single-round variant of iterative self-improvement methods such as STaR~\citep{zelikman2022star}. The procedure is straightforward: the SFT model generates $N{=}8$ candidate outputs per training document, each candidate is scored by CED, and only the top-$k{=}1$ response is retained as training data. We use $k{=}1$ (best-of-$N$) selection throughout all experiments, selecting the single highest-scoring candidate per document for RSFT training data construction. This yields approximately 3,000 training examples per model (one per document; exact counts vary slightly by model due to generation failures). Unlike GRPO and DPO, RSFT does not apply gradient-based optimization pressure toward a reward signal. Instead, it improves training data quality through filtering: the model trains on its own best outputs rather than all outputs. This distinction is critical because discrete selection over complete outputs cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off that gradient methods find. The contribution of RSFT in our setting is not the framework itself, which is well-established, but its application to evidence-grounded DocRE with CED as a domain-specific quality filter.


\subsection{Contrastive Evidence Discrimination}
\label{sec:ced}

CED is a scoring function that evaluates the faithfulness of predicted evidence sentences to their associated relation triples. It builds on Natural Language Inference (NLI) to measure whether evidence actually entails the predicted relation, rather than merely co-occurring with the relevant entities.

For a predicted triple $(e_h, r, e_t)$ with evidence sentences $E$, CED constructs a positive NLI pair: the premise is the concatenation of sentences in $E$, and the hypothesis is a natural-language verbalization of the relation (e.g., ``$e_h$ has relation $r$ with $e_t$''). An NLI model (\texttt{cross-encoder/nli-deberta-v3-base}) produces an entailment probability $p^+$ for this pair. The contrastive component introduces hard negatives drawn from the same document. For each triple, we collect sentences that mention $e_h$ or $e_t$ but are not in the predicted evidence set. These entity-sharing distractors serve as negative premises, and the NLI model scores each to obtain $p^-_1, \ldots, p^-_m$. The CED score for a single triple is:
\begin{equation}
\text{CED}(e_h, r, e_t, E) = p^+ - \max_j \, p^-_j
\label{eq:ced}
\end{equation}
This formulation forces the scoring function to distinguish sentences that genuinely support the relation from sentences that merely mention the same entities. A high CED score indicates that the evidence provides relation-specific support beyond entity co-occurrence.

The document-level CED score aggregates over all predicted triples, weighted by extraction correctness: triples that match ground-truth relations contribute their CED scores, while incorrect triples receive zero credit. This coupling ensures that CED rewards both faithful evidence and accurate extraction jointly.

We also evaluate a \emph{flat-NLI} baseline that uses only the positive entailment score $p^+$ without the contrastive term. CED and flat-NLI produce comparable RSFT outcomes (mean Rel-F1 difference of 0.02pp across available seeds; Section~\ref{sec:analysis}), confirming that the contrastive formulation is not essential for data selection quality. We adopt CED as the default because its per-triple discrimination scores support interpretable error analysis.


\subsection{Evidence-Decoupling Ratio}
\label{sec:edcr}

We introduce the Evidence-Decoupling Ratio (EDCR) as a diagnostic metric that quantifies the degree of misalignment between a model's extraction and evidence capabilities. EDCR is not a training component; it serves purely as an analytical tool for comparing optimization methods.

EDCR is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\text{EDCR} = \frac{\text{Evi-Recall}_{\text{TP}}}{\text{Rel-Recall}}
\label{eq:edcr}
\end{equation}
where $\text{Evi-Recall}_{\text{TP}}$ is evidence sentence recall computed only over correctly predicted triples (true positives), and $\text{Rel-Recall}$ is overall relation extraction recall. The numerator captures evidence quality on the subset of triples the model gets right; the denominator captures how many triples the model identifies in total.

The intuition is as follows. A model that correctly identifies a few triples and provides excellent evidence for them, but misses most triples entirely, will have high $\text{Evi-Recall}_{\text{TP}}$ and low $\text{Rel-Recall}$, yielding a high EDCR. This pattern signals that evidence quality has decoupled from extraction capability: the model produces faithful evidence only within a narrow comfort zone.

EDCR operates at two diagnostic levels. At the method level, comparing EDCR across seven independent method configurations reveals a negative correlation with Rel-F1 (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.89$, $n{=}7$, $p{=}0.007$). Because EDCR shares Rel-Recall with Rel-F1 by construction, this correlation should be interpreted as a diagnostic pattern rather than an independent discovery; its primary value lies in per-relation analysis. We validate EDCR's diagnostic utility through per-relation analysis (\S\ref{sec:analysis}), which avoids the shared-component concern present in cross-method correlation. At the per-relation level, computing EDCR for individual relation types reveals a robust negative correlation with Rel-F1 ($\rho{=}{-}0.79$, $n{=}76$, $p{<}0.001$) that is independent of Evi-F1 ($\rho{=}{-}0.06$, $p{=}0.58$). This independence means EDCR captures relation-specific extraction-evidence misalignment that aggregate evidence metrics miss. The SFT baseline EDCR (0.6982) reflects the inherent cost of evidence generation; EDCR values substantially exceeding this level (e.g., GRPO QLoRA at 0.9876) indicate RL-induced decoupling beyond what evidence production alone explains, while values below the baseline (e.g., RSFT at 0.6503) reflect improved co-optimization.

\subsection{Evidence-Extraction Decoupling Spectrum}
\label{sec:spectrum}

Because evidence and extraction share gradient capacity, different optimization paradigms handle this competition in qualitatively different ways. We organize the outcomes along a \emph{decoupling spectrum} from catastrophic collapse to no decoupling, shown in Table~\ref{tab:spectrum} (Figure~\ref{fig:spectrum} in Appendix).

\paragraph{GRPO QLoRA: catastrophic collapse.}
Under 4-bit quantization ($G{=}4$), GRPO drives Rel-F1 to 1.38 while Evi-F1 rises to 85.04 (EDCR 0.9876): the model produces well-formed evidence but loses extraction capability. This extreme decoupling co-occurs with constrained precision; full-precision GRPO (below) avoids it.

\paragraph{GRPO bf16: mild, variable.}
Full-precision GRPO ($G{=}8$) avoids catastrophe, with the best checkpoint achieving Rel-F1 45.49, Evi-F1 80.86, and EDCR 0.6318 (below SFT baseline 0.6797). Both sub-tasks improve jointly, though the variable training trajectory requires careful checkpoint selection.

\paragraph{DPO: gradual decay.}
DPO progressively trades extraction for evidence: Rel-F1 drops from 40.34 (1 epoch) to 34.29 (3 epochs)\footnote{DPO 3-epoch results use an earlier evaluation protocol; included for trend analysis.} while evidence improves (Evi-F1 80.88$\to$84.03, EDCR 0.7139$\to$0.7294).

\paragraph{RSFT: no decoupling.}
RSFT maintains both capabilities simultaneously (Rel-F1 45.80, Evi-F1 80.96, EDCR 0.6487) because discrete output selection cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off that gradient methods find.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Evidence-extraction decoupling across RL paradigms (Qwen3-4B). Severity indicates the degree of extraction collapse relative to evidence improvement.}
\label{tab:spectrum}
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Paradigm} & \textbf{Config} & \textbf{Rel-F1} & \textbf{Evi-F1} & \textbf{EDCR} & \textbf{Severity} \\
\midrule
GRPO & QLoRA $G{=}4$ & 1.38 & 85.04 & 0.9876 & Catastrophic \\
GRPO & bf16 $G{=}8$ & 45.49 & 80.86 & 0.6318 & Mild \\
DPO & bf16 1ep & 40.34 & 80.88 & 0.7139 & Gradual \\
DPO & bf16 3ep$^\dagger$ & 34.29 & 84.03 & 0.7294 & Gradual \\
\midrule
SFT & baseline$^\ddagger$ & 44.39 & 79.61 & 0.6797 & None \\
RSFT & CED$^\ddagger$ & 45.80 & 80.96 & 0.6487 & None \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}%
}
\vspace{-2mm}
{\raggedright\footnotesize $^\dagger$Earlier evaluation protocol. $^\ddagger$Three-seed mean. Severity reflects training trajectory stability (propensity for extraction collapse during optimization), not the final-checkpoint EDCR value.\par}
\end{table}


\subsection{Scale-Dependent Evidence Tax}
\label{sec:scaling}

The evidence tax, defined as Ign-F1(no-evidence) $-$ Ign-F1(with-evidence) on the development set, is scale-dependent. Under matched evaluation conditions (\texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048, format-compliant intersection; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_tax}), the training-time tax reveals a within-family capacity threshold: evidence generation benefits 1.7B ($-$5.63pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_tax}), where it also halves the output duplication rate (60.5\%$\to$33.0\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:error_analysis}), but is approximately neutral at $\geq$4B (+0.25$\pm$0.47pp at 4B, $n{=}3$; +0.04pp at 8B; +0.03pp at 14B). Table~\ref{tab:scaling} presents RSFT performance and inference-time evidence tax across five models (Figure~\ref{fig:scaling} in Appendix).

RSFT navigates this capacity landscape through quality-based data selection rather than gradient pressure. Mean Ign-F1 improves across tested scales: +6.55pp at 1.7B, +1.24pp at 4B, +4.33pp at 8B, and +3.79pp at 14B (Table~\ref{tab:scaling}). RSFT from an independent SFT base (s44, Method B) achieves 51.52 Ign-F1 at 4B (+4.18pp over that base). This result confirms that RSFT gains are not contingent on a specific SFT checkpoint.

Beyond mean performance, RSFT substantially improves training reliability. Three RSFT seeds from a shared SFT checkpoint yield standard deviations of 0.09pp at 1.7B, 0.51pp at 4B, and 0.19pp at 8B ($n{=}3$ each), compared to SFT cross-seed variation of 2.79pp, 3.80pp, and 3.23pp respectively. While these measure different sources of variance (SFT reflects full training runs from different random seeds; RSFT reflects additional fine-tuning from a fixed base), the practical consequence is direct: variance compression is 31$\times$ at 1.7B, 7.5$\times$ at 4B, and 17$\times$ at 8B. The 1.7B RSFT model (51.19 Ign-F1, std 0.09pp) approaches 8B SFT performance (50.19, std 3.23pp) at 4.7$\times$ fewer parameters---more accurate in mean and 36$\times$ more predictable.

Cross-family validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B confirms these patterns. All three RSFT seeds (55.87--57.14) exceed all three SFT seeds (48.02--53.84), yielding complete pairwise seed separation (Cohen's $d{=}2.58$) and 4.6$\times$ variance compression (std 2.92pp $\to$ 0.64pp). The SFT seed lottery is starkest at s44 (48.02 Ign-F1, 24.4\% truncation, elevated Evi-F1 85\%), a degenerate outcome that RSFT eliminates entirely (s44: 55.87). This robustness extends to inference-time format changes: LLaMA SFT models evaluated without evidence at 1024 tokens suffer 38--42\% truncation with Ign-F1 collapsing to ${\sim}$0.33 (s43/s44), while RSFT models truncate only 18--19\% with stable performance (55.41$\pm$0.13pp), indicating that CED filtering selects for compact output structure.

For RSFT models, the inference-time evidence tax (same model evaluated with vs.\ without evidence output) preserves the benefit direction at 1.7B ($-$7.75pp, confirmed with 2048-token no-evidence evaluation that rules out truncation). At 4B, where the training-time tax is approximately neutral (+0.25$\pm$0.47pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:training_tax}), the RSFT inference-time tax is similarly small (+0.94pp). At 8B, the tax reverses to a benefit ($-$0.78pp), and at 14B the benefit is largest ($-$4.54pp). RSFT thus maintains or improves extraction quality with evidence across the Qwen3 family. LLaMA-3.1-8B RSFT shows a larger inference-time evidence benefit ($-$2.17$\pm$0.76pp, three-seed mean, vs.\ $-$0.78pp for Qwen3-8B), confirming that both performance gains and training reliability generalize across model families.

